From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hwcomposer: add an external display on a second compositor

The HAL drives one display on one Wayland connection. Dual-screen
handhelds have a second panel with its own compositor, and Android apps
reach a second screen through the external display and the Presentation
API.

When waydroid.wayland_display_ext names a socket, open a second
connection to it with one fullscreen toplevel and report it as
HWC_DISPLAY_EXTERNAL, hotplugged once the client has registered its
callbacks. SurfaceFlinger composites that display; its framebuffer target
is what gets presented.

The second connection binds the seat for touch only. EventHub has a
fixed list of input pipes and all of them belong to the primary display,
so the first touch point is written to the otherwise unused tablet pipe,
scaled to the axis range EventHub reports for it. An input device
configuration file for wayland_tablet associates it with the external
display.

With config_localDisplaysMirrorContent false, Android leaves the external
display empty until an app presents there. While it is empty, drop the
toplevel's role so the second compositor can show its own content or power
the panel down; a null buffer alone would leave the window geometry on an
empty surface, which wlroots rejects. The role comes back, through a new
configure, once an app presents there. The toplevel starts out unmapped, and
the display is announced once Android has booted, after framework overlays
apply, so it never shows a mirror on the way up.

The HAL also never waited for the compositor to release a buffer: every
layer got release fence -1 and the retire fence was already signalled at
set time. hwc2on1adapter frees the previous client target on the retire
fence, so SurfaceFlinger could draw into a buffer still being scanned out,
which shows as black flicker on part of the screen. Each buffer now gets a
sync timeline advanced on wl_buffer.release; layers get release fences on
it, and the retire fence is the client target's release fence, which the
adapter hands back one set later. A buffer still held is not attached
again, so a queued release cannot signal the newer use, and buffers
dropped from the cache live on until released.

